`SWBUtil.OSLog` wraps `os.OSLog` and declares itself `Sendable`, which only
holds if `os.OSLog` is `Sendable` too. Apple declared it so in a later SDK than
the one this toolchain pins (apple-sdk_14, whose `os` module is
user-module-version 1049.100.4), so add the conformance retroactively, gated the
same way swift-tools-protocols gates its `OSSignpostIntervalState` conformance.

--- a/Sources/SWBUtil/OSLog.swift
+++ b/Sources/SWBUtil/OSLog.swift
@@ -15,6 +15,15 @@
 #if canImport(os)
 public import os
 
+// Apple declared `os.OSLog` as `Sendable` in a later SDK than the one this
+// toolchain pins. Without the conformance the wrapper below cannot be
+// `Sendable` either, and `log(_:)` cannot capture it. Matches the pattern
+// swift-tools-protocols uses for `OSSignpostIntervalState`; the guard drops it
+// again once the pinned SDK is new enough.
+#if !canImport(os, _version: 1062.100)
+extension os.OSLog: @retroactive @unchecked Sendable {}
+#endif
+
 /// A wrapper around the OSLog.
 public final class OSLog: Sendable {
 
